# Git commits

## Local Cursor (interactive)

- **Never** create commits yourself (`git commit`, `git add` + commit, etc.).
- When changes are ready to commit, **always** provide the exact command(s) for the user to run.
- Do not ask whether to commit first — just supply the command after finishing the work.
- Follow the repo’s existing commit-message style when drafting the message.

## Cloud Agent

- Create commits yourself as you go, using the repo’s existing commit-message style.
- After completing a task, **push** any new branches and commits to the remote so progress is visible to the developer working on the updates (`git push -u origin <branch>` for a new branch).
- Do not leave completed work only on the local checkout.

## Pull request descriptions

- Write PR titles and bodies in the **present tense imperative**, matching commit style: “Add …”, “Update …”, “Correct …”.
- Prefer “Add Discord schedule updates for …” over “Adds Discord schedule updates for …”.
- Prefer “Update …”, “Correct …”, “Document …” over “Also updates …”.
- Bullet lists of included changes should also use present tense where they start with a verb.
- In Discord schedule-update PR bodies, **markdown-link the club/group name** (not the whole bullet) to the live group page:
  `https://botc-events.uk/clubs/{group_id}/`.
  - `{group_id}` is the club file’s `group_id`, or the filename without `.md` when `group_id` is omitted.
  - Example: `[Gillingham Social Club](https://botc-events.uk/clubs/gillingham-gillingham-social-club/)`.
  - When the bullet uses a place or venue phrase instead of `name:` (e.g. “Saffron Walden”, “Halifax Shadowdust”), link that phrase to the same club URL.
  - Still link newly created groups; the page may 404 until the change is deployed.
